{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And your dread.</b> Your fear — as in ״תִּרְאוּ חֲתַת״ (Job 6). And the aggadah: חִתְּכֶם is a term of liveliness — as long as an infant a day old is alive, you need not guard it from the mice; Og king of Bashan has died — you need to guard it from the mice, as it is said ״וּמוֹרַאֲכֶם וְחִתְּכֶם יִהְיֶה״ — when will your dread be upon the beasts? As long as you are alive (Shabbat 151)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The aggadah plays on reading וחתכם as connected to חיה, 'alive'; the pun underlies Rashi's phrase לשון חיות."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:04.262903688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}